# Cursor Rules — BlockFlip

## Project Context
BlockFlip is a Solana RWA protocol for property flipping (Auction → Renovation → Sale).
Stack: Next.js 16 App Router, TypeScript strict, Tailwind CSS v4, Shadcn/ui, Solana Web3.js.
UI Style: Elite fintech (Brex/Ramp), Dark Mode, Solana Green #14F195.
Goal: Top 1 Global at Colosseum Hackathon.

## Code Standards
- Always use TypeScript strict mode — no implicit `any`
- Components: PascalCase | Functions: camelCase | Constants: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE | Files: kebab-case
- Validate all user inputs with Zod
- Use async/await pattern consistently
- Keep components under 200 lines — split if larger
- Prefer Shadcn/ui components over custom implementations

## Security First (OWASP Top 10)
- Never expose private keys, seeds, or wallet data
- Always sanitize inputs before processing
- Maintain security headers in middleware.ts
- Implement rate limiting on all API routes
- Never log sensitive user data (wallet addresses in logs only as needed)

## UI/UX Rules
- "Brutal Clarity": hide blockchain complexity from end user
- Never use raw addresses in user-visible text without truncation + explorer link
- Maintain dark theme with #14F195 accent consistently
- Financial data: always show in USDC with proper formatting

## Solana Specific
- Use @solana/wallet-adapter-react for wallet connections
- Never access window.solana directly — use wallet adapter hooks
- Always check wallet connection before transaction actions
- Use devnet for development, mainnet for production

## Before Committing
1. Run: npm run type-check
2. Run: npm run build
3. Run: npm run lint
4. Verify: no sensitive data in diff
5. Verify: middleware.ts security headers intact

## File Structure
src/app/ → routes | src/components/ → UI | src/lib/ → utils/security/validations
src/data/ → mock data | src/types/ → TypeScript types | src/hooks/ → custom hooks
